el.jpg","w":2560,"h":1920,"ok_print":true},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Otacive_hlediste_Cesky_Krumlov.jpg/960px-Otacive_hlediste_Cesky_Krumlov.jpg","thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Otacive_hlediste_Cesky_Krumlov.jpg/500px-Otacive_hlediste_Cesky_Krumlov.jpg","caption":"Otáčivé hlediště Český Krumlov","author":"Szombat78","licence":"CC0","licence_url":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otacive_hlediste_Cesky_Krumlov.jpg","ok_print":true}],"intro":{"lead":"UNESCO medieval town, river-bend castle","body":"Český Krumlov (German: Krumau) is a beautiful town of 14,600 inhabitants in Southern Bohemia, in the Czech Republic. Like Prague, the town is situated on the Vltava River and is full of Baroque buildings housing cafes and bars, features a spectacular castle (the second largest in the Czech Republic), and an old-town square. The town's appearance is little changed since the 18th century and the buildings have been well maintained and restored. In 1992, Cesky Krumlov was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List. The town is very popular among tourists, who outnumber the local population in the summer and are still a notable presence during Christmas time.","grounding":[{"source":"wikivoyage","title":"Český Krumlov","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/%C4%8Cesk%C3%BD_Krumlov","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"facts":{"population":12797,"visit_h":6}},"highlights":[{"id":"hl-cesky-krumlov-castle","name":"Český Krumlov Castle","kind":"Castle","lat":48.81083,"lon":14.31535,"dist_km":0.1,"rank_score":11.48,"resolved_by":"catalogue","fact":"Castle in the Czech Republic","image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/%C4%8Cesk%C3%BD_Krumlov%2C_z%C3%A1mek_cel%C3%BD_z_vyhl%C3%ADdky.jpg/500px-%C4%8Cesk%C3%BD_Krumlov%2C_z%C3%A1mek_cel%C3%BD_z_vyhl%C3%ADdky.jpg","author":"VitVit","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licence_url":"h